Kawasaki disease
Paediatric & Neonatal
Fever > 5 days, Strawberry tongue/lip cracking, Polymorphous rash/extremity changes

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-028
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting high fever for 6 days accompanied by a spreading rash and severe mucosal changes, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out toxic shock syndrome or severe drug eruption.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a child with a prolonged, high-spiking fever unresponsive to paracetamol, a diffuse rash, and distinctly red, cracking lips. Immediate assessment was required to definitively diagnose acute systemic vasculitis (Kawasaki disease) prior to the next in-hours period to ensure intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG) therapy can be initiated within the critical 10-day therapeutic window to prevent coronary artery aneurysms.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "His fever hasn't dropped below 39 degrees for six days, his lips are bleeding, and his whole body is covered in a blotchy red rash."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an unremitting high fever explicitly persisting for six days, completely unresponsive to standard antipyretics. The timeline reflects a progressive systemic inflammatory response: the fever began on day 1, followed by bilateral eye redness on day 3, and erythema and cracking of the lips on day 4. A polymorphous, generalized, non-vesicular maculopapular rash prominently involving the perineal area erupted yesterday. Today, the caregivers note erythema and induration of the palms and soles. The infant demonstrates extreme irritability completely out of proportion to a standard viral illness.
Functional Status: Highly irritable, inconsolable, refusing solid food.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of irreversible cardiac complications.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 145 | Temp: 39.4C | RR: 30 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 14 Kg
General: Markedly irritable, toxic-appearing, flushed, tachycardic.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals prominent erythema and cracking of the lips, accompanied by a severe, classic strawberry tongue. Ocular examination shows bilateral, non-exudative, painless bulbar conjunctival injection explicitly sparing the limbus. Cutaneous inspection confirms a polymorphous maculopapular rash heavily concentrated in the groin. The palms and soles are distinctly erythematous and indurated, without current desquamation. A unilateral, firm cervical lymph node measuring approximately 2.0 cm is palpable on the right. The diagnostic tetrad of fever > 5 days, strawberry tongue/lip cracking, and polymorphous rash with extremity changes is unequivocally confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying listlessly on the couch; multiple empty antipyretic bottles nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: Maximum doses of ibuprofen and paracetamol given with zero effect on the fever.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Kawasaki Disease - Status: Acute phase (High risk for coronary aneurysm)
Differentials:

Scarlet Fever (Group A Strep) - Ruled out by the presence of prominent bilateral conjunctivitis, lack of exudative tonsillitis, and the lack of response to empirical amoxicillin if previously started.

Toxic Shock Syndrome - Ruled out by the maintenance of normal blood pressure (absence of profound shock) and normal renal output.

Adenovirus Infection - Ruled out by the severe lip cracking, distinct induration of the extremities, and the extreme duration of the unyielding fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical (High medical risk of cardiovascular morbidity)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of prolonged high fever, mucosal inflammation, and extremity changes mandated an immediate, critical after-hours assessment. Telephone triage identified the classic hallmark symptoms of Kawasaki disease, an acute systemic vasculitis. Objective clinical evaluation definitively confirmed the diagnostic criteria while ruling out bacterial toxic shock and severe drug eruptions. Delaying this assessment would have pushed the patient closer to or beyond the critical 10-day window for intravenous immunoglobulin administration, resulting in an unacceptably high risk of permanent, lethal coronary artery aneurysms. Immediate diagnosis allowed for the immediate initiation of an emergency hospital transfer for definitive, life-saving cardiovascular therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Maintained hemodynamic stability. Coordinated immediate, direct admission to the pediatric medical ward for urgent echocardiography and administration of Intravenous Immunoglobulin (IVIG) and high-dose aspirin.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instruct the parents to completely cease all attempts to forcefully feed the highly irritable child solid foods tonight, offering only small, frequent sips of cool water via a syringe. Rewarding the child's compliance with small fluid intakes without the pressure of solid food directly reduces the severe pain caused by the lip cracking and oral inflammation, lowering generalized distress during the transfer process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to the inpatient pediatric team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via private vehicle (if stable) or call 000 if the patient develops chest pain, severe shortness of breath, sudden pallor, or loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Kawasaki disease is a severe inflammatory condition requiring inpatient cardiovascular monitoring.
Clinical Course: Without immediate IVIG treatment, up to 25% of children develop permanent swelling of the blood vessels supplying the heart. Treatment dramatically reduces this risk.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the hospital pediatric ward as arranged. Do not delay. Call 000 en route if the child becomes unresponsive or struggles to breathe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The child has a rare but serious condition that causes severe inflammation of the blood vessels throughout the entire body, including the heart. The fever will not respond to normal medications. The child must receive specialized antibodies through an IV drip in the hospital immediately to protect their heart.
Actionable Steps: Do not give any more ibuprofen or paracetamol. Gather your belongings and proceed directly to the emergency department for admission.

